    {
      slug: "bank-rate-energy-shock-sterling-gold",
      kind: "Timely analysis",
      evergreen: false,
      title: "The Bank raised rates. That is not a simple signal for sterling gold.",
      dek: "The November vote took Bank Rate to 4%. The minutes put a scale on the energy move, and they do not treat the vote as a path the Committee shares. For gold in pounds, currency translation is a separate sum.",
      description: "What the Bank of England's November 2026 decision, energy-futures averages and split minutes do and do not say for sterling gold. Not investment advice.",
      checked: "5 November 2026, 21:46 UK (GMT)",
      asOf: "Checked against the Bank of England\u2019s 5 November 2026 Monetary Policy Summary, minutes and Monetary Policy Report at 21:46 UK (GMT) on 5 November 2026. This note is not a live price feed. The futures averages are for the 15 UK working days ending 27 October 2026. September 2026 CPI is an outturn. The 2027 inflation figures are the Bank\u2019s November projections, not outcomes.",
      sources: [
        { name: "November 2026 Monetary Policy Summary and Minutes", url: "https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/monetary-policy-summary-and-minutes/2026/november-2026", note: "Summary; minutes paragraphs 3, 16 and 19\u201322; the entries for Andrew Bailey, Huw Pill, Alan Taylor and Catherine L Mann. Checked 5 November 2026, 21:46 UK (GMT)." },
        { name: "November 2026 Monetary Policy Report", url: "https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/monetary-policy-report/2026/november-2026", note: "Overview and Chart 1.1; indirect effects around Chart 1.3; section 1.3; Box A. Checked 5 November 2026, 21:46 UK (GMT)." }
      ],
      blocks: [
        { h: null, ps: [
          "The recorded decision is narrow, and it is easy to read it as something larger. At the meeting ending 4 November 2026 the Monetary Policy Committee voted 8\u20131 to raise Bank Rate by 0.25 percentage points, to 4%. One member, Catherine L Mann, voted for an increase of 0.5 percentage points, to 4.25%. The <a href=\"https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/monetary-policy-summary-and-minutes/2026/november-2026\">Monetary Policy Summary and minutes</a>, published on 5 November 2026, state that vote in the summary and again in paragraphs 19\u201322.",
          "A reader of gold in pounds can turn that into a rule: higher rates help sterling and hurt gold. The minutes do not establish that rule. They do establish the scale of the energy move the Committee was looking at, and they show that members did not agree what should happen next."
        ] },
        { h: "The scale is a futures average, not a spot price on the day", ps: [
          "Minutes paragraph 3 gives the energy comparison the Committee was using. The front-month Brent crude oil futures contract and the UK front-month natural gas futures contract averaged $108 per barrel and 216 pence per therm, respectively, over the 15 UK working days ending 27 October. Those averages were 38% and 81% higher than at the time of the July Monetary Policy Report, and 5% and 27% above the July Report\u2019s adverse-scenario assumptions for 2026 Q4 futures prices. The \u201crespectively\u201d attaches 38% and 5% to Brent, and 81% and 27% to UK gas.",
          "That is not a spot print from 4 or 5 November, and it is not a price at which a reader could have dealt. Paragraph 3 also says spot and futures prices had risen further. It does not publish a separate spot level in that sentence. Anyone who quotes $108 or 216 pence as \u201cthe\u201d price on decision day is changing the Bank\u2019s wording.",
          "Inflation has a published outturn and a separate projection. The summary says UK CPI inflation increased to 3.5% in September. Chart 1.1 of the <a href=\"https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/monetary-policy-report/2026/november-2026\">Monetary Policy Report</a> dates that outturn to September 2026. The same chart projects inflation of around 5% in 2027 Q2. The Report says CPI inflation is projected to average 4.9% in 2027 Q2. The summary\u2019s \u201caround 5% by the middle of next year\u201d is that projection, not a result. Mann\u2019s minutes entry cites projected CPI inflation of 4.9% by mid-2027 and says the projection is conditioned on a significant decline in energy prices."
        ] },
        { h: "Limited second-round evidence is not the same as a finished shock", ps: [
          "The summary separates the direct rise in energy bills from knock-on effects. It says there remains limited evidence of knock-on effects on other prices and wages, and that pressure still in the pipeline, with activity stronger than expected, warranted the increase.",
          "Box A of the Report is more precise than a comfort blanket. It says there continues to be little evidence of second-round effects in the available data, and that this does not rule out stronger second-round effects later. It says the risk of those effects has increased.",
          "The cushion from contracts and margins is a different passage. Around Chart 1.3 the Report says firms\u2019 electricity and gas costs have not yet risen much, partly because of fixed-price contracts and hedging, and that some firms have absorbed cost rises in lower margins. Andrew Bailey\u2019s minutes entry says the same mechanisms \u201ccan only be temporary\u201d. An earlier version of this note placed that cushion inside Box A. Box A does not say it."
        ] },
        { h: "The vote was shared. The next step was not", ps: [
          "Paragraph 19 records that eight members preferred the 0.25 percentage point increase. It also says there was a range of views among those eight on how far the earlier disinflation had gone, how much spare capacity remained, and how restrictive policy already was. That is a range of judgements about the present stance. It is not a published range of future Bank Rate levels.",
          "The individual entries disagree about what follows. Huw Pill writes that, of itself, \u201ca single 25-basis point hike is unlikely to be sufficient\u201d, and that more tightening is likely to be required, including in the near term. Alan Taylor writes that, as of now, \u201cthis is not the start of a hiking cycle\u201d, and that from an already restrictive position \u201cthis is a risk-management \u2018one-for-now\u2019 insurance hike\u201d. He then writes: \u201cThe next move could be up, down, or sideways.\u201d Those sentences are his minutes entry, not a Committee line and not a phrase we have smoothed into \u201cconditional and precautionary\u201d.",
          "Mann preferred 4.25% at this meeting. Her entry treats the mid-2027 projection as depending on energy prices falling, and says similar futures curves earlier in the year had not come about. Reading the 8\u20131 vote as a settled view that rates will keep rising, or as a settled view that they will not, drops one of those entries."
        ] },
        { h: "A higher curve is not a promise of that path", ps: [
          "Section 1.3 of the Report says the market curve contains both expected Bank Rate and risk premia. It reports that the median response to the November 2026 Market Participants Survey had Bank Rate at 4% in November, 4.25% in February, and 3.5% two and three years ahead, and that the curve sat above that survey path because of risk premia. Bailey\u2019s entry makes the same distinction: some of the upward slope is risk premia, not expected rates.",
          "Those are the Bank\u2019s November descriptions of a curve and a survey. They are not a live yield, and they are not gold\u2019s discount rate. Bank Rate itself is a nominal policy setting. If expected inflation moves with it, the real-return comparison for an asset that pays no interest need not move by 0.25 percentage points."
        ] },
        { h: "Sterling and dollar gold are different numbers", ps: [
          "A dollar gold reference price becomes a sterling reference price by dividing by the pound\u2019s dollar price, at the same time. If dollar gold were unchanged, a stronger pound would lower that sterling figure and a weaker pound would raise it. If dollar gold also moved, both changes would count. Spreads, charges and any hedge would move a real holding again.",
          "The Report describes a possible channel in which higher UK rates can lift sterling and then lower import prices. It also notes that this exchange-rate channel has not always been the one that moved inflation. The November decision does not measure how far sterling or dollar gold moved on the day, and it does not say which way they must move. UK government bond yields are a place those expected rates and risk premia can show up. They are not, by themselves, a real yield, and this note quotes no gilt yield and no sterling rate."
        ] },
        { h: "Three cases already in the minutes, and what would test them", ps: [
          "Paragraph 16 sets out three cases, and says they would call for different policy if they materialised. The central projection assumes more second-round effects than in July because energy prices are higher. The adverse case is persistently higher energy prices and larger second-round effects. The weaker-demand case is growth giving way, with more spare capacity limiting those effects. The table uses those cases. It is a way to watch the Bank\u2019s own tests. It is not a forecast, and it does not rank the cases.",
          "<div class=\"c101h-watch\"><style>.c101h-watch{margin:8px 0 0}.c101h-watch table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse}.c101h-watch caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;font-weight:600;padding:0 0 8px}.c101h-watch th,.c101h-watch td{text-align:left;vertical-align:top;padding:8px 12px 8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule,#dedad2);font-size:1rem;line-height:1.45}.c101h-watch th{font:600 12px/1.3 var(--sans,system-ui);letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase}@media (max-width:640px){.c101h-watch thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0)}.c101h-watch tr{display:block;padding:12px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule,#dedad2)}.c101h-watch td{display:block;border:0;padding:2px 0 8px}.c101h-watch td::before{content:attr(data-h);display:block;font:600 11px/1.3 var(--sans,system-ui);letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted,#5f6368);margin-top:6px}}</style><table><caption>Watching paragraph 16. Not a forecast.</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Case in the minutes</th><th scope=\"col\">What to watch</th><th scope=\"col\">What would count against it</th><th scope=\"col\">What it can and cannot say for gold</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-h=\"Case in the minutes\">Adverse case: energy prices stay high and second-round effects build.</td><td data-h=\"What to watch\">Front-month Brent futures and UK front-month gas futures, compared with the 15 working days to 27 October, and surveys of inflation expectations.</td><td data-h=\"What would count against it\">Those futures averages fall back, or expectations do not follow headline inflation.</td><td data-h=\"What it can and cannot say for gold\">Higher energy costs can lift near-term inflation and, if pay and prices follow, the real-return comparison. They do not set the sterling price of gold.</td></tr><tr><td data-h=\"Case in the minutes\">Central projection: the energy pressure eases enough to validate the path the Bank has drawn.</td><td data-h=\"What to watch\">The same energy futures, UK government bond yields, and sterling against the dollar.</td><td data-h=\"What would count against it\">Futures stay near the late-October averages, or yields and the pound move for another reason.</td><td data-h=\"What it can and cannot say for gold\">Easier expected policy and lower risk premia would show in the curve described in section 1.3. A change in sterling changes the pound value of an unchanged dollar gold price only as arithmetic. The minutes give neither size nor sign.</td></tr><tr><td data-h=\"Case in the minutes\">Weaker demand: activity fades and spare capacity limits second-round effects.</td><td data-h=\"What to watch\">UK activity, pay, and the gap between the market curve and surveys of expected Bank Rate.</td><td data-h=\"What would count against it\">Activity and the labour market stay resilient, as the summary says they recently have, and pay starts to respond.</td><td data-h=\"What it can and cannot say for gold\">More slack would, on the Bank\u2019s account, lean against second-round effects. Gold in pounds would still depend on dollar gold and the exchange rate, which this case does not decide.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
        ] }
      ]
